    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96489,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96489.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, that seems like it was so long ago, but I'm a practicing lawyer, and so I've been representing renewable energy companies and infrastructure projects in the state, and I work for a law firm called Fox Rothschild. We just merged with Fox Rothschild at the end of last year. I was formerly with Smithmore Leatherwood. They had offices in four cities in North Carolina. And I taught at UNC, which is how I met you. One of the first fellows for the nascent Institute of Politics and I taught a seminar on economic security and social mobility and the building blocks of economic security and social mobility. We brought in some great speakers and I loved working with the students and have kept in touch with a fair number of them including you since then and have enjoyed my association with the university. I also worked on a lot of campaigns. I was on Anita Earl's Finance Committee and did some stumping for her around the state and helped several of our candidates helped break the majority. So I've been moving around and there are a few nonprofits I've helped too. I helped the Burning Coal Theater Company here in Raleigh and the Green Chair Project here in Raleigh. So I've been having a good time and I got a dog.",
