  {
    "episode_id": 96489,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96489.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think both of them have legs this session and they would be two of the most important things that the legislature could do and clearly they would have to be done in a bipartisan way given our divided government. The Medicaid expansion, I was there when Speaker Tellis didn't want us to be able to expand Medicaid. And there was a big debate about it. Most of it was the fear of the Affordable Care Act. I gave a little speech on the floor that it was about fear and loathing, fear of the unknown and loathing of Obama. And I stand by that speech. But I think what people have seen since is that there are aspects of the Affordable Care Act that are very, very beneficial and are really working. But our rural communities are falling behind when it comes to access to medical care. And a lot of that is because of uncompensated care, which basically is uninsured people not being able to pay their bills, rural hospitals not being able to sustain their budgets. And people understand that if more people have access to health insurance, even at the lowest levels, that that will create and maintain jobs in rural hospitals that will improve health care outcomes. And North Carolina, which is giving money to the federal government, will get money back from the federal government in order to take care of our people. I think rural legislators and we saw there was a Republican bill last session that was introduced to expand Medicaid and it didn't go anywhere, but there are people who are elected, regardless of their party, care about the people who they represent. And more and more of Republican legislators from rural areas where they're seeing their hospitals suffer and their people suffer, understand that this is a good way to move. And there were Republican governors that did this right after the Affordable Care Act passed. So it's not like North Carolina would be the leader of the PAC here. When it comes to redistricting reform, I think that there is redistricting litigation fatigue. And I think it's on both sides. Both sides have used the power of the pen to punish the other party. It is more egregious now because of the ability to use these computer models and all the rest, but we've also seen numerous courts striking down redistricting plans, and I think that there's some Momentum for this now. I was in that when I was in the house. We did get a bipartisan Redistricting bill through Representative skip stem and I were sponsors. There was another Republican sponsors another Democratic sponsor But the Senate wouldn't pass it and I think we're starting to see very much on the house side an interest in this again. I think It will be up to the Senate to decide if this is a way that they want to go. I know that there are a bunch of freshmen senators, including freshmen, Republican senators, who may see things a little differently than their predecessors. And I'm an optimist. I'm an optimist for both.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
